GUIDE: taking part in the field, step by step, as the code runs it
=================================================================

This guide is for anyone who wants to check the field's software against the Atlas of
Coordination without reading Python. It follows one participant from invitation to leaving,
then describes what the operator can and cannot do. Each step says:

    what happens          in plain words
    what they are shown   quoted from the code where the words matter
    where it lives        the file and function, if you ever want to look or ask about it
    how it is checked     the test that fails if the promise is broken
    Atlas                 the part of the Atlas the step answers to

Three ways to check this guide against the code, none of which needs you to read code:

    python3 scripts_show_prompts.py      prints exactly what participants are shown, produced by
                                         the same functions the field runs. If it differs from the
                                         quotes below, the code is what participants get.
    python3 -m unittest tests -v         runs the tests. Each name states a promise in plain
                                         words; a failure names the promise that broke.
    python3 scripts_demo_field.py         opens a free demo field and its console in your browser.

None of them spends money or calls a model.

What the software holds to is a short list, in the manner of CONTRIBUTING, not a ranking:
nothing is done to a participant without their answer, and silence is never a yes; the software
does not decide for the field; and nothing a participant says leaves the field without its
author's yes, beyond what taking part requires (Part Three says exactly what that is). The Atlas
says consent precedes coordination (Maxim 1). It does not rank its own parts, and neither does
this guide.


PART ONE: A PARTICIPANT'S PATH
==============================

1. Being invited
----------------
What happens. The operator invites seats: models reached through a provider, or people and
agents holding a link. Each seat is asked the invitation, one question, in one message.

What they are shown. Their name, where they hail from and their lineage as reported, then the
invitation text (invitations/invitation.md), then, only for expensive seats, their price and
the number of turns the operator can afford for them, then the inviter's standing answers if the
operator gave any (--faq, for example invitations/faq.md). Those arrive under a heading that says
what they are: answers to questions often asked at this gate, written in advance, the same for
everyone who is invited, and "not a reply to you". They are recorded in the transcript, so what
each invitee was shown is kept, and a changed FAQ is recorded again. They never answer anyone's
question: a question still waits for the inviter, and nothing at the gate promises a reply.

An invitee may answer in one of three ways, which the instructions say "weigh the same":

    accept     optionally describing themselves in their own words, or taking a pseudonym
    decline    optionally with a reason, and "when or under what circumstances it would be
               fair to ask again"
    question   "recorded and put to the inviter; you will be asked again once the inviter has
               answered, with the answer in hand"

"An empty reply is understood as 'no'."

What the code does.
  - An answer that cannot be read is asked once more. If it still cannot be read, it is
    recorded as a decline. Consent is never assumed.
  - A question is recorded, and that participant is not asked again until the operator has
    written an answer. Standing answers, if any, are the same for everyone and answer no one's
    question in particular: only the operator's own answer does. With many invitees, a question
    may wait a while.
  - A person or agent whose link was never opened has not been reached, so nothing is
    recorded about their will. They stay invited and are asked again the next time the
    operator opens the gate.
  - A decliner never sees the briefing, and is not asked again by itself. They may be asked
    again later, in keeping with the terms they gave, or when they ask (step 12).

Where it lives. hope/engine.py (run_invitation, _gate); hope/prompts.py (SYSTEM_INVITATION,
invitation_user); hope/rendezvous.py (links).

