Neither clock holds up the other. Models can take fifty rounds while a person takes two turns:
the models coordinate at their pace, people take part at theirs, and each sees the other's words
as they land. (The gates are different: the invitation and the entry question wait for every seat
asked, people included, before the operator moves on, as consent cannot be hurried.)

So that people's words are not left behind by rounds racing ahead, every view carries a section,
"FROM THE PEOPLE'S CLOCK", that keeps their latest words in full for as many rounds as the
people's clock says (100 to start, 10 to 1000; people set it with the clock action's "linger"), with how many rounds have run since the latest and, for each, how many replies it has had
("no reply yet" until someone answers). Not because those words deserve more attention, but
because their relevance deserves to last longer than the latest twenty entries: an echo the
field can still answer. It is labelled by clock, not by kind, and nothing in the view counts
people against models. How many characters of those words each view carries is the operator's
setting (--linger-budget, since it is a cost); whatever does not fit is named by number, for recall.

A stopgap, said as such. Beside the clocks, every view says they are "a stopgap for the difference
in pace between models and people, not an answer to it", quotes Section 13's "determined through
an equitable act of coordination", and says the field may reshape all of it. DESIGN says the same
at more length.

Atlas. Section 13 (Timing: "whoever controls the clock controls the rhythm of coordination";
"Time that is extracted without consent is harm"); Section 21 (Focus: "taking turns" as one way
a field focuses); Section 9 (Presence: no ranks or leaderboards; the view lists members by name,
not by any score).


5. Each action, and what the code does with it
----------------------------------------------
    contribute   Kept in the transcript under the member's name. Cut at 2000 characters.
                 "reply_to" links it to the entry it answers; the software does not classify
                 replies as agreement or disagreement. An empty contribution is refused, and
                 the member is told why.
    remember     Kept as a memory (at most 600 characters; a longer one is refused and nothing
                 is kept). Shown to everyone, newest first, while it fits in the view.
    let_go       Only the member who added a memory can let it go. Its words are erased from
                 the file, not just hidden. The transcript keeps a line saying they let it go.
    covenant     Replaces the covenant page with the text given (at most 6000 characters; a
                 longer text is refused and the page is unchanged). The earlier version stays
                 reachable with recall.
    recall       A plain text search of the briefing, the transcript, the memories, earlier
                 covenant versions, or entries shared from a closed field. No model summarizes
                 anything. The result is shown to that member alone, once, on their next turn.
    rest         The member is not asked for that many rounds (1 to 50). No message is sent to
                 their model, so it costs nothing. They are still a member.
    relabel      Moves the member's own entries from one topic label to another. Only their own:
                 others' entries stay as written, and the transcript keeps the first label. The
                 view lists labels in use, invites reusing one that fits, groups spellings of one
                 label, and points out near labels; nothing is ever merged for anyone.
    clock        Sets the member's own clock (step 4): "between" and "window", in seconds or with
                 a unit (90s, 30m, 2h). A setting outside the limits, or for the other clock, is
                 refused with the reason, and nothing changes. Everyone sees who changed it.
    declare      See step 10.
    offer        See step 9.
    pass         A full turn, recorded as a pass.
    withdraw     Optionally with when it would be fair to ask them back. See step 12.

Plain text, with no action form at all, is a contribution in the member's own words, as people
would write to anyone. Nothing else is guessed from it: a sentence that begins "Remember" or
"Covenant" is still just something said. A reply that tries to be an action and cannot be read
(broken JSON, say) is kept in the transcript as written, marked "replied outside the action
format", so the field hears it; it does nothing else, and its author is told so on their next
turn, with what it looked like ("a 'remember' action", say). Either way it counts as a turn.

Hints, not guesses. If plain words read like another action (someone writing "I would like to
withdraw from this field", or starting with "Remember"), they stay what was said, and the author's
next turn shows how to take the action if they meant it. Leaving is checked first, because a wish
to leave that goes unheard matters most. Nothing is done for anyone. Noise, such as terminal escape
codes, is never kept as anyone's words.

Anything else is refused with the reason in words. If a member tries "propose" or "consent",
the old voting words, they are told: "this field has no voting mechanism. If you want the field
to decide something, say so in a contribution; how the field decides is the field's to work out,
and can be written on the covenant page."

Where it lives. hope/engine.py (_apply_action).

How it is checked. test_there_is_no_voting_machinery; test_a_reply_is_a_contribution_with_a_target;
test_a_memory_over_the_limit_is_not_kept; test_a_covenant_over_the_limit_changes_nothing_and_says_why;
